 man stood at little more  than five foot five  by the way if a hard-nosed northern  irish copper being sent into birmingham  to deal with the peaky blinders is  ringing a few bells that'll be because  it's pretty much exactly what happens in  the first couple of series of the tv  show stephen knight has never publicly  confirmed that sam neil's character  inspector campbell  was based on rafter but the similarities  are hard to ignore  whatever the truth rafter's method soon  began to pay dividends  he used his growing army of giant  brawlers and police uniforms to  overpower the local gangs and within 10  years or so the peaky blinders had all  but ceased to exist  but our story isn't over just yet  because not all the peaky blinders were  quite so ready to hang up their knuckle  dusters and as birmingham's somewhat  disorganized gang scene crumbled into  rafters iron grip  one young hard man saw an opportunity to  fill that void with a brand new crime  wave of a rather more organized variety  his name was billy kimber if you're a  fan of the show you'll know him as the  leader of the birmingham boys and tommy  shelby's first major rival in the series  but whilst the real william kimber was  indeed the head honcho of the birmingham  boys you might be surprised to learn  that he actually started out life as a  peaky blinder from the mean streets of  birmingham in the show he's portrayed as  a londoner not only that but there's  plenty of similarities between billy  kimber's real rise to power and tommy  shelby's fictional one  under kimber's leadership the birmingham  boys grew to become one of britain's  first genuine organized crime empires  focusing mainly on illegal butt making  and extortion much like tommy shelby did  with his peaky blinders billy kimber  quickly pushed the boundaries of his  empire  far beyond birmingham eventually taking  over race courses across most of central  and northern england  by the early 1920s kimber was very  probably the most powerful gangster in  britain but there was one area of the  country his influence didn't yet reach  london  just like tommy shelby kimber was an  insatiably ambitious man  and so in 1921 he decided to make his  move on the capital the birmingham boys  went after the bookmakers of london  using intimidation tactics in an attempt  to wrestle control of the lucrative  racecourses that can be found in and  around the city it was going pretty well  too  until one of kimber's men picked on the  wrong bookmaker alfie solomon  again that name's going to be familiar  to fans of the show  though the real alfie solomon an s was  added to the character's name for the  show  wasn't quite the major crime lord he's  portrayed as on tv by tom hardy though  he did have strong ties to london's  jewish gangs  anyway to cut a long story short  kimber's goons beat seven shades of [ __ ]  out of poor old alfie solomon  leaving him with lacerations across his  face and several missing teeth  solomon was incensed obviously and he  took his grievance against the  birmingham boys to the so-called  godfather of the east end a jewish  gangster by the name of edward emanuel  solomon convinced emanuel that the  birmingham boys were a threat and  emmanuel used his prodigious influence  to set legendary english italian mob  boss charles darby sabini another  prominent character in the tv show and  london's racecourse kingpin against  billy kimber  after several large-scale skirmishes the  first gang wars to make national  headlines in britain  sabini eventually emerged victorious  regaining control of the london  racecourse scene kimber was allowed to  keep his tracks in the midlands and the  north but it's rumored he eventually  made his way to america where he worked  for none other than al capone though  details of his activities there are a  little sketchy what we do know is that  he lived out his later years in england  where he left behind his criminal past  to become a legitimate bookmaker d